  "answer": "This has been reported before. It is a known limitation of Keyboard Manager, and the archive shows no fix. A disabled shortcut only blocks that exact key combination. If you hold Alt+Tab and press another key such as W, the combination no longer matches the remap, so Windows handles Alt+Tab normally. In PowerToys#44306 a commenter said this is by design. PowerToys#25034 was closed as a duplicate of PowerToys#21850, which is the open request for a 'fallthrough' option that would also block these extra-key combinations. The only workaround in those tickets is to add a separate Disabled remap for each specific combination, for example Alt+W+Tab. That covers combinations of up to 3 keys, because shortcuts are limited to 2 modifiers plus 1 action key. It can't cover every combination. You can follow or upvote PowerToys#21850 to ask for a proper fix."
 },
